</pre>

== Information audit ==

[[Information]] remains deliberately multi-sense.

The article separates:

* probabilistic/Shannon information;
* data representation;
* semantic/content notions;
* physical/computational embodiment.

The page does not claim that Shannon information alone captures truth, usefulness, or meaning.

== Information-theory audit ==

[[Information theory]] remains mathematical.

Its Core quantities include entropy, conditional entropy, mutual information, source coding, channel capacity, and rate–distortion structure.

The article explicitly preserves:

<pre>
information theory ≠ theory of semantics
</pre>

Shannon's formalism is used for uncertainty/coding limits rather than semantic interpretation.

== Entropy audit ==

This is the principal cross-domain boundary in Wave 9.

The pages preserve:

<pre>
I(x) = -log p(x)

H(X) = -Σ p(x) log p(x)

S_Gibbs = -k_B Σ p_i ln p_i
</pre>

The Shannon and Gibbs/Boltzmann forms share logarithmic probability structure.

The article does not claim literal physical identity between Shannon entropy and thermodynamic entropy.

“Disorder” is retained only as a sometimes-useful intuition, not the definition.

== Feedback audit ==

[[Feedback]] is defined through a causal loop in which prior output/state influences later system behavior.

The pages preserve:

<pre>
negative feedback ≠ bad feedback
positive feedback ≠ good feedback
response ≠ feedback without a loop
</pre>

Stability is not inferred from feedback sign alone.

Delay, gain, poles/dynamics, nonlinearities, and saturation remain relevant.

== Emergence / self-organization audit ==

[[Emergence]] and [[Self-organization]] remain linked but distinct:

<pre>
emergence
→ higher-level property/pattern relative to lower-level description

self-organization
→ decentralized process producing/maintaining organized pattern
</pre>

Emergence is not treated as inexplicability or supernatural novelty.

Self-organization does not require isolation from environmental energy or constraints.

== Complex-system audit ==

[[Complex system]] does not use a fashionable-trait checklist as a definition.

The page states that interactions matter strongly to collective behavior and then presents nonlinearity, feedback, emergence, heterogeneity, adaptation, multiscale structure, and networks as common characteristics rather than universal necessary/sufficient conditions.

The article preserves:

<pre>
complex ≠ merely large
complex ≠ merely complicated
</pre>

== Network audit ==

[[Network]] is the general relational concept.

[[Computer network]] remains the technological subtype.

The pages preserve the modeling distinction:

<pre>
graph
→ abstract mathematical node–edge structure

network
→ empirical/modeled relational system, often represented by a graph
</pre>

The terms can be interchangeable in some mathematical contexts without being strict identity claims across all scientific use.

== Robustness audit ==

[[Robustness]] is explicitly perturbation- and function-relative.

A valid robustness claim must specify:

<pre>
robust to what?
which structure/function is preserved?
</pre>

The page distinguishes robustness from:

* resilience;
* reliability;
* invulnerability.

The 2024 Nature Reviews Physics complex-network review supports the current robustness/resilience distinction and the dependence of network robustness on topology and dynamics.

== Adaptation audit ==

[[Adaptation (systems)]] stays broader than one mechanism.

It can involve:

* feedback;
* learning;
* parameter adjustment;
* structural reconfiguration;
* behavioral change;
* evolution;
* policy change;
* network rewiring.

The page preserves:

<pre>
adaptation ≠ biological evolution by definition
adaptation ≠ machine learning by definition
adaptation ≠ explicit global optimization by definition
</pre>

Adaptation and robustness are linked but non-identical.

== Cross-wave audit ==

Wave 9 connects cleanly to earlier concepts:

* [[Probability]] / [[Probability distribution]] ground information-theoretic quantities without being redefined.
* [[Semantics]] remains separate from Shannon information.
* [[Computer network]] remains a subtype/neighbor of general [[Network]].
* [[Knowledge graph]] and [[Semantic network]] remain specialized informational networks.
* [[Distributed system]] remains a computational architecture, not a synonym for complex network.
* [[Learning]] remains cognitive/general learning rather than systems adaptation as a whole.
* [[Generalization (machine learning)]] and [[Distribution shift]] connect to robustness/adaptation without becoming their definitions.
* [[Autonomous agent (artificial intelligence)]] can use feedback/adaptation while remaining an AI-agent concept.
* [[System boundary]] remains essential because complexity, network, feedback, and robustness claims depend on modeled system scope.

No Wave 9 page requires redefining an earlier canonical concept.

== Public concept versus local/project application ==

The wave preserves:

<pre>
information        ≠ repository metadata
information theory ≠ token counting
entropy            ≠ one dashboard randomness score
feedback           ≠ user-feedback form
emergence          ≠ unexpected agent behavior
self-organization  ≠ unmanaged process
complex system     ≠ complicated codebase
network            ≠ one blockchain/network
robustness         ≠ “passed our tests”
adaptation         ≠ fine-tuning only
</pre>

No local architecture defines a public concept.

